  { question: "What income level qualifies for Lifeline in 2026?", answer: "In 2026, Lifeline income eligibility is based on gross household income at or below 135% of the Federal Poverty Guidelines. The dollar amount depends on household size and whether the household is in the 48 contiguous states/DC/territories, Alaska, or Hawaii." },
  { question: "Does Medicare qualify for a free tablet?", answer: "Medicare alone is not listed as a regular Lifeline qualifying program. A senior with Medicare may still qualify through Medicaid, SSI, SNAP/EBT, Veterans Pension, Survivors Benefit, FPHA, income-based Lifeline, or another accepted route." },
  { question: "Do seniors qualify for free tablets?", answer: "Seniors may qualify if they meet Lifeline income rules or participate in a qualifying program such as Medicaid, SSI, SNAP/EBT, Veterans Pension, Survivors Benefit, or FPHA. Age alone or Medicare alone does not guarantee a tablet." },
  { question: "Do students qualify for free tablets?", answer: "Students may qualify through household income or accepted Lifeline qualifying programs. School, Pell, or library programs may help in local or special survivor contexts, but they should not be treated as regular general Lifeline qualifiers without official confirmation." },
  { question: "Do veterans qualify for free tablets?", answer: "Veterans Pension and Survivors Benefit can qualify for Lifeline. VA Health Care alone should not be presented as a regular Lifeline qualifier. Tablet availability still depends on provider terms and local availability." },
  { question: "Do disabled people qualify for free tablets?", answer: "Disabled users may qualify through SSI, Medicaid, SNAP/EBT, FPHA, income, or another accepted route. Disability alone may not be enough, and a tablet is not guaranteed." },
  { question: "Can more than one person in my household get a tablet?", answer: "Do not assume every family member can receive a Lifeline-supported device. Lifeline generally allows one benefit per household, and any phone or tablet offer is subject to provider terms, stock, and eligibility review." },
  { question: "What is the Lifeline one-per-household rule?", answer: "A household generally means people living together and sharing income and expenses. Lifeline generally allows one benefit per household, not one per person. Roommates, shelters, nursing homes, or separate economic households may need a Household Worksheet." },
  { question: "What documents do I need to prove eligibility?", answer: "Common documents include government ID, proof of address or acceptable location information, proof of SNAP/EBT, Medicaid, SSI, FPHA, Veterans Pension, Survivors Benefit, Tribal benefit, income proof, household worksheet, or other identity details if requested." },
  { question: "Can people experiencing homelessness apply?", answer: "People experiencing homelessness may still be able to apply using a temporary address, shelter address, or descriptive location where allowed. They should follow official Lifeline, state, or provider instructions for address verification." },
  { question: "Do domestic violence or trafficking survivors have special Lifeline rules?", answer: "Survivors of domestic violence, human trafficking, or related crimes may have special temporary Lifeline support rules and extended criteria when they meet official requirements. These survivor rules should be kept separate from general Lifeline eligibility." },
  { question: "Are Tribal lands benefits different?", answer: "Qualifying Tribal lands may have additional Lifeline support and Tribal program eligibility routes, including Bureau of Indian Affairs General Assistance, Tribal TANF, Head Start with income standard, and Food Distribution Program on Indian Reservations. This does not guarantee a tablet." },
  { question: "Did ACP end?", answer: "Yes. ACP and EBB are no longer current benefit routes for new free tablet eligibility. Users should check current Lifeline, provider, school, library, VA, nonprofit, state, or local digital inclusion options instead." },
  { question: "Does eligibility guarantee a free tablet?", answer: "No. Lifeline eligibility does not guarantee a tablet. Tablet availability depends on provider stock, ZIP code, state rules, plan terms, documents, shipping, activation, and possible co-pay." },
  { question: "Which providers should I compare after checking eligibility?", answer: "Compare AirTalk Wireless, Assurance Wireless, SafeLink Wireless, StandUp Wireless, and any local providers by ZIP code. Verify device options, tablet stock, BYOP rules, data, shipping, activation, possible co-pay, and current official terms." },
  { question: "Is FreeTablet.click the government or a provider?", answer: "No. FreeTablet.click is independent. We are not the FCC, USAC, Lifeline, National Verifier, a government agency, or a wireless provider. We do not approve applications, issue benefits, manage accounts, or ship devices." },
];

